# GitHub Local Index (repo workspace)

When working in this repository:

1. **Read first:** `.github-local-indexer/INDEX.md` or `status.json`
2. **Check cache age:** `last_indexed_at`, `index_age_minutes`, `auto_sync_after_minutes`, and `pending_jobs`
3. **Auto-enqueue stale refresh:** if any registered source collection is older than 24 hours and no source job is pending/running, call `sync owner/repo` without `--wait`; report the stale collections and job ID
4. **Search locally:** `.cursor/bin/github-local-indexer search "<query>" --json`
5. **Use the CLI only:** do not call GitHub API directly; automatic sync is allowed only through the CLI after the threshold above

## Refresh cache (agent)

```bash
# Check if refresh needed
.cursor/bin/github-local-indexer status --json

# Enqueue a refresh asynchronously (worker needs GITHUB_TOKEN)
.cursor/bin/github-local-indexer sync owner/repo

# Add repo then sync (first time)
.cursor/bin/github-local-indexer repo add owner/repo
.cursor/bin/github-local-indexer sync owner/repo --wait
```

Auto-enqueue only when a collection's `index_age_minutes` > `auto_sync_after_minutes` (1440 by default). Use collection ages because the source-level age records the newest local write and can hide stale collections. A stale marker at the 15-minute freshness TTL is still useful for reporting, but does not trigger an automatic refresh. Do not enqueue duplicate work when source `pending_jobs` is nonzero. The CLI `sync` command only records a durable job; the current release has no always-on daemon, so report “queued” rather than “refreshed” until a worker processes it and the job succeeds.
